) Contribution Limits for 2026: The Complete Guide","og:type":"website","og:url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/guides/401k-contribution-limits-2026/","twitter:card":"summary_large_image","twitter:description":"The 2026 401(k) contribution limit is $24,500 ($32,500 if you're 50+, $35,750 if you're 60-63). See the Roth/Traditional combined cap and catch-up rules.","twitter:image":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon-192.png","twitter:title":"401(k) Contribution Limits for 2026: The Complete Guide","viewport":"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"},"schema":[{"@context":"https://schema.org","@id":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/#organization","@type":"Organization","description":"Free, accurate financial calculators — auto loans, mortgages, retirement, investing, rentals, and net worth. No signup, instant results.","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/favicon.svg"},"name":"ModernWallet","parentOrganization":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Layer3Labs","url":"https://www.layer3labs.io"},"url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/"},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@id":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/#website","@type":"WebSite","description":"Free, accurate financial calculators — auto loans, mortgages, retirement, investing, rentals, and net worth. No signup, instant results.","name":"ModernWallet","url":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/"},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","item":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/","name":"ModernWallet","position":1},{"@type":"ListItem","item":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/guides/","name":"Guides","position":2},{"@type":"ListItem","item":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/guides/401k-contribution-limits-2026/","name":"401(k) Contribution Limits for 2026: One Combined Cap for Roth and Traditional","position":3}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The 2026 401(k) contribution limit is $24,500 for workers under 50. Workers 50 and older can add an $8,000 catch-up for $32,500 total, and workers 60 to 63 get an $11,250 catch-up for $35,750 total."},"name":"What is the 401(k) contribution limit for 2026?"},{"@type":"Question","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The $24,500 limit for 2026 applies across your Roth and Traditional 401(k) contributions combined, not to each account separately. Contributing $15,000 to Traditional leaves $9,500 of room for Roth, not another full $24,500."},"name":"Do Roth 401(k) and Traditional 401(k) contributions share the same limit?"},{"@type":"Question","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Your employer's match counts toward a separate, higher combined limit of $72,000 for 2026, which covers your own deferrals plus employer contributions together. Only your own paycheck deferrals count toward the $24,500 cap."},"name":"Does my employer's 401(k) match count toward my $24,500 limit?"},{"@type":"Question","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Workers 50 and older can add $8,000 on top of the $24,500 base limit, for $32,500 total. Workers 60 to 63 get a bigger $11,250 catch-up under the SECURE 2.0 Act, for $35,750 total."},"name":"What is the 401(k) catch-up contribution for 2026?"},{"@type":"Question","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You have to withdraw the excess deferral, plus any earnings it made, by April 15 of the following year. Miss that deadline and the excess amount gets taxed twice: once in the year you contributed it, and again when you eventually withdraw it."},"name":"What happens if I contribute more than the 401(k) limit?"}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Person","description":"Jonathan Velez writes and edits ModernWallet's personal-finance content, focused on loans, interest, and debt payoff. Every calculator page is reviewed against primary sources like the CFPB and Federal Reserve.","jobTitle":"Personal Finance Writ