t.com/compare/","name":"Compare","position":2},{"@type":"ListItem","item":"https://www.themodernwallet.com/compare/roth-401k-vs-traditional-401k/","name":"Roth 401(k) vs Traditional 401(k): Which Saves More?","position":3}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Choose Roth 401(k) if you're early in your career and expect a higher tax rate in retirement. Choose Traditional 401(k) in your peak earning years when your current rate is high. Many financial planners recommend splitting contributions to hedge tax uncertainty. For more detail, see 401(k) vs Roth IRA."},"name":"Should I choose Roth or Traditional 401(k)?"},{"@type":"Question","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, if your employer's plan allows it. You can split your contributions between Roth and Traditional in any proportion, as long as the combined total doesn't exceed the annual limit ($24,500 in 2026). For more detail, see 401(k) Contribution Limits for 2026."},"name":"Can I have both a Roth and Traditional 401(k) in the same plan?"},{"@type":"Question","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. Roth 401(k) contributions are made with after-tax money, so they don't reduce your current year's taxable income. Traditional 401(k) contributions do reduce your taxable income."},"name":"Do Roth 401(k) contributions reduce my taxable income?"},{"@type":"Question","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The Roth 401(k) has no income limits and a much higher contribution limit ($24,500 vs $7,500 in 2026). The Roth IRA often has more investment options. Most people should use both: max the Roth IRA, then use the Roth 401(k) for additional savings."},"name":"Is the Roth 401(k) better than a Roth IRA?"},{"@type":"Question","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Roth 401(k) money comes out fully tax-free only as a qualified distribution, which generally means a 5-year participation period has passed and the 